  "pitfalls": [
    "Case mix drives every outcome: a unit that treats sicker patients shows higher mortality, length of stay and cost. Compare like with like (same specialty, diagnosis or admission type) before ranking (Simpson's paradox).",
    "Rates here are crude, not risk-adjusted, so they are not comparable with published standardised mortality or readmission ratios.",
    "Encounter exports often repeat the admission on every charge, diagnosis or procedure line: count admissions and patients with DISTINCT IDs, never by counting rows.",
    "Recent periods are incomplete: patients still admitted have no discharge date and claims are still being decided, so stay length, readmissions and denials look better than they will.",
    "Small departments and rare diagnoses swing wildly; treat rates from fewer than about 30 admissions as noise.",
    "Bed counts are snapshots: add them across wards on one day, never across days."
  ]
}
